CAT = "nzsfeTiriti"; SEG = "TIR"
Q = [
("Under the cultural safety framework taught in Aotearoa, who decides whether care was culturally safe?",
 "The person receiving care", ["The nurse who gave the care", "The charge nurse on duty", "The hospital interpreter"],
 "Cultural safety is judged by the person receiving care, not by the nurse: the 2025 standards say culturally safe care is determined by the recipient. The nurse's task is to reflect on how their own culture and power shape that care."),
("In Te Whare Tapa Whā, what does taha wairua describe?",
 "Spiritual health", ["Physical health", "Family and social health", "Mental health"],
 "The four walls are taha tinana (physical), taha hinengaro (mental and emotional), taha whānau (family and social) and taha wairua (spiritual), with whenua, the place where you stand, as the foundation."),
("A nurse asks Mere how her thoughts and feelings have been since her diagnosis. Which wall of Te Whare Tapa Whā is she assessing?",
 "Taha hinengaro", ["Taha tinana", "Taha wairua", "Taha whānau"],
 "Taha hinengaro is mental and emotional wellbeing: thoughts, feelings and mood. Taha tinana is the body and taha wairua the spirit."),
("Which of the Te Tiriti principles used for the health system supports Māori having kaupapa Māori services to choose from?",
 "Options", ["Equity", "Active protection", "Partnership"],
 "The options principle asks for properly resourced kaupapa Māori services and for all services to be culturally appropriate, so Māori have real choices about their care."),
("Which of the Te Tiriti principles used for the health system guarantees Māori self-determination in the design and delivery of their own health services?",
 "Tino rangatiratanga", ["Active protection", "Options", "Equity"],
 "Tino rangatiratanga is self-determination and mana motuhake in the design, delivery and monitoring of services. Active protection is the duty to act to achieve equitable outcomes for Māori."),
("What is kawa whakaruruhau?",
 "Cultural safety for Māori", ["A tikanga for burial", "A model of family therapy", "A form of rongoā medicine"],
 "Kawa whakaruruhau is cultural safety in a Māori context: care that upholds the identity, values and rights of Māori and protects their mana."),
("Koro is dying and twelve members of his whānau arrive on the ward in the evening. What is the most culturally safe response?",
 "Arrange a space for the whānau", ["Allow only two visitors at once", "Ask them to return in the morning", "Call security to clear the bay"],
 "Whānau gathering around a dying relative is central to tikanga Māori. The nurse finds a whānau room or suitable space and works with them, while keeping other patients' needs in mind."),
("Which practice follows common tikanga guidance in a hospital?",
 "Separate head and body cloths", ["One basin for food and washing", "Pillows used as seat cushions", "Hair combed over the meal tray"],
 "The head is tapu and food is noa, so they are kept apart. Separate cloths for the head and the lower body, and keeping pillows off chairs and food surfaces, show respect."),
("After a Māori patient dies, the whānau ask that the tūpāpaku not be left alone. What should the nurse do?",
 "Support whānau to stay with the body", ["Move the body to the mortuary at once", "Ask whānau to wait in the corridor", "Explain that policy forbids it"],
 "Tūpāpaku is the body of the person who has died. Many whānau keep a presence with the tūpāpaku, and the nurse supports this and their karakia while completing care."),
("After birth, a Māori mother asks to take her baby's placenta home. What should the nurse do?",
 "Return it to the whānau safely", ["Send it to the laboratory", "Dispose of it as clinical waste", "Refuse because of infection risk"],
 "The whenua (placenta) is often returned to the land. Health services give it to the whānau in a suitable container with advice on safe handling."),
("Which long-term condition affects Māori and Pacific people in New Zealand at higher rates and younger ages?",
 "Type 2 diabetes", ["Coeliac disease", "Multiple sclerosis", "Cystic fibrosis"],
 "Type 2 diabetes is more common and starts earlier among Māori and Pacific peoples, an inequity nurses address through screening, education and whānau-centred care."),
("Who developed the Te Whare Tapa Whā model of health?",
 "Sir Mason Durie", ["Irihapeti Ramsden", "Florence Nightingale", "Te Rangi Hīroa"],
 "Sir Mason Durie, a leading Māori health advocate, developed Te Whare Tapa Whā in 1984. Its four walls stand on whenua."),
("In Te Whare Tapa Whā, what does whenua represent?",
 "Connection to the land", ["Spiritual health", "The wider family", "The body"],
 "Whenua is the place where you stand: connection to the land, a source of life and wellbeing and a place of belonging. Taha wairua is spiritual health."),
("Who developed cultural safety in New Zealand nursing education in the 1980s?",
 "Irihapeti Ramsden", ["Sir Mason Durie", "Whina Cooper", "Te Puea Hērangi"],
 "Irihapeti Ramsden developed cultural safety (kawa whakaruruhau) in nursing education in the 1980s. It was written into New Zealand nursing training in 1992."),
("A Tongan family prefers to discuss their father's care in Tongan. Who should interpret?",
 "A trained health interpreter", ["The patient's 12-year-old son", "A Tongan cleaner on the ward", "The nurse with phrase cards"],
 "Right 5 of the Code of Rights includes a competent interpreter where needed. Children and untrained staff may omit or soften clinical information and breach privacy."),
("Cultural safety first asks a nurse to reflect on what?",
 "Their own culture and power", ["The patient's religion", "The patient's ethnicity", "The ward's visiting hours policy"],
 "Cultural safety starts with the nurse understanding their own cultural identity and the power imbalance between nurse and patient, rather than learning lists about other cultures."),
("In the Nursing Council's standards, whakapapa-centred care focuses on whose needs?",
 "Person, whānau and future generations", ["The patient alone, without whānau", "The admitting doctor and the team", "The hospital and its funders"],
 "Whakapapa-centred care meets the needs, values and outcomes of individuals, whānau and future generations, recognising the long-term impact of care."),
("Whānau ask to say a karakia before Hemi goes to theatre. What should the nurse do?",
 "Allow time for the karakia", ["Refuse, as theatre is waiting", "Ask them to pray in the car park", "Suggest it after surgery"],
 "Karakia gives spiritual support at an anxious time. Allowing a few minutes respects taha wairua and rarely delays the list."),
("In the 2025 registered nurse standards of competence, what is pou one?",
 "Māori health", ["Cultural safety", "Rangatiratanga and leadership", "Manaakitanga"],
 "The six pou are Māori health, cultural safety, whanaungatanga and communication, pūkengatanga and evidence-informed practice, manaakitanga and people-centred care, and rangatiratanga and leadership."),
("Giving more support to groups with worse health outcomes so outcomes become fair is an example of:",
 "Equity", ["Equality", "Paternalism", "Rationing"],
 "Equality gives everyone the same; equity gives according to need so that outcomes become fair, which is the aim for Māori health under Te Tiriti."),
]
